oquo:CardinalToCardinalScalePointMapping
        a                owl:Class ;
        rdfs:comment     "Represents the mapping of a numeric interval of a cardinal scale onto another cardinal scale by means of a linear function (output = slope * input + intercept)." ;
        rdfs:label       "cardinal to cardinal scale point mapping" ;
        rdfs:subClassOf  oquo:ScalePointMapping ;
        rdfs:subClassOf  [ a                         owl:Restriction ;
                           owl:onClass               oquo:Interval ;
                           owl:onProperty            oquo:hasInputCardinalInterval ;
                           owl:qualifiedCardinality  "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger
                         ] ;
        rdfs:subClassOf  [ a                         owl:Restriction ;
                           owl:onDataRange           xsd:double ;
                           owl:onProperty            oquo:hasIntercept ;
                           owl:qualifiedCardinality  "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger
                         ] ;
        rdfs:subClassOf  [ a                         owl:Restriction ;
                           owl:onDataRange           xsd:double ;
                           owl:onProperty            oquo:hasSlope ;
                           owl:qualifiedCardinality  "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger
                         ] ;
        obo:IAO_0000112  "A metric whose raw values range in the interval [0, 1] can be converted into a percentage scale from 0 to 100 with a single mapping with input interval [0, 1], slope 100 and intercept 0. A lower-best metric in [0, 1] can be converted into the same higher-best percentage scale with slope -100 and intercept 100. A metric returning counts can be capped at 100 with two mappings: [0, 10) with slope 10 and intercept 0, and [10, inf) with slope 0 and intercept 100." ;
        obo:IAO_0000115  "A scale point mapping that converts values of an input cardinal scale into values of an output cardinal scale through a linear function defined over a numeric interval. Each mapping has exactly one input cardinal interval (hasInputCardinalInterval), one slope (hasSlope) and one intercept (hasIntercept): a raw value x that falls within the input interval is converted into the value y = slope * x + intercept on the output scale. A cardinal to cardinal scale conversion may contain one or several of these mappings, thus defining a piecewise linear function. A single mapping covering the whole input scale represents a plain linear transformation (e.g. a rescaling or a change of units), whereas several mappings allow different behaviours in different ranges of the input scale, such as saturating the output beyond a threshold by using a slope of 0. A negative slope inverts the direction of the scale, which can be used to convert a lower-best input scale into a higher-best output scale. The input intervals of the mappings of a conversion should not overlap and should cover the range of the input scale, and the resulting values should fall within the boundaries of the output scale." .

<https://purl.org/oquo-oquare#scalePointMapping-8d345512-756b-4c49-9285-aaeb332acfe4>
